--TEST--
A child isolate answers no request of its own, so `Core\Request` and `Core\Session` throw inside one while its parent goes on answering the request that spawned it, and `Core\Server::isDraining` answers anyway because the drain bit is process state — `rule:security/request-state-throws-in-an-isolate`, `rule:security/isolate-shares-nothing`
--GET--
page=2
--FILE nvs.toml--
# `rule:security/capability-check-at-the-door`: `spawn script` goes through a door that asks for
# `script.spawn` first, and deny-by-default means a case with no configuration
# cannot spawn at all. This grants it and nothing else.
[capabilities.script]
spawn = true

# A store is chosen, so that the child's `Core\Session::start` is refused for the
# reason this case is about and not for an unconfigured tree. Nothing ever reaches
# it: `start` asks whether there is a request before it asks anything about where
# a record would live, and the message asserted below is how the child says which
# question stopped it.
[session]
backend = "shared"
--FILE child.nvs--
<?nvs
// `rule:security/isolate-shares-nothing`: this program shares the compiled unit
// with the one that spawned it and nothing else. That the parent is answering a
// request is not a fact reachable from here, and the refusals below are how the
// child is told so — passing the request down automatically would be ambient
// authority crossing the boundary that exists to stop it.
uint $refused = 0;
try {
    var $verb = Core\Request::method();
} catch (LogicError $none) {
    $refused = $refused + 1;
}

// The same question one class over, and the one that would otherwise *succeed*:
// a deployment that has a store would mint a record and write a cookie for a
// caller that can never present either. `[session] backend` is written in this
// tree, so a child that got as far as the store question would say so in the
// message, and the line below reads the message rather than the count.
string $said = "";
try {
    Core\Session::start();
} catch (LogicError $nothing) {
    $refused = $refused + 1;
    $said = $nothing->message;
}

echo "request state refused ", $refused, " of 2\n";
echo "and it refused for the absent request rather than the absent store: ",
    Core\Str::contains($said, "there is no request here") ? "yes" : "no", "\n";

// The bound asserted on the other side. `Core\Server::isDraining` is not request
// state: it reads the process's drain bit, which a child, its parent and a CLI
// program all share, so it answers instead of refusing — a child that refused
// here would be reporting that a shutdown question has no answer in an isolate.
echo "the process is still readable: draining=",
    Core\Server::isDraining() ? "true" : "false", "\n";
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The parent is answering a request — the `--GET--` section above is that
// request — which is what makes the child's refusals a property of the boundary
// rather than of this process. A case run with no request at all cannot tell
// those two apart, because every line of it would refuse.
echo "the parent reads its own request: page=",
    Core\Request::query("page") ?? "", "\n";

var $handle = spawn script "child.nvs" with(output: "capture");
var $child = await $handle;

// `rule:security/isolate-failure-is-a-value`: what the child did arrives as data.
// It ran to its end past both refusals, so this is the success row and its whole
// buffer crossed with it.
echo "ok=", $child->ok ? "true" : "false", "\n";
echo $child->output;

// And the request is still the parent's afterwards: the child asking for it and
// being refused consumed nothing from the context that has one.
echo "the parent still reads its own request: page=",
    Core\Request::query("page") ?? "", "\n";
--EXPECT--
the parent reads its own request: page=2
ok=true
request state refused 2 of 2
and it refused for the absent request rather than the absent store: yes
the process is still readable: draining=false
the parent still reads its own request: page=2
